lib: sbi_pmu: a stop with RESET frees a counter that is already stopped

Linux's riscv_pmu_del() stops a counter and then stops it once more with
SBI_PMU_STOP_FLAG_RESET to release it. The second call found the counter
stopped, returned SBI_EALREADY_STOPPED before the reset, and the counter
stayed taken: every perf event that ended used one up for good.

--- a/lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c	2026-10-05 00:44:01.730001069 +0900
+++ b/lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c	2026-10-05 00:44:01.738001069 +0900
@@ -718,7 +718,15 @@
 		else
 			ret = pmu_ctr_stop_hw(cidx);
 
-		if(ret)
+		/*
+		 * A counter that is already stopped is still freed by a stop
+		 * with RESET: Linux's riscv_pmu_del() stops the counter and
+		 * then stops it again with RESET to release it, and the second
+		 * call used to return here and leave the counter taken for
+		 * good (mmRISC-2, CPU_CORE_SPEC.md decision 69).
+		 */
+		if (ret && !(ret == SBI_EALREADY_STOPPED &&
+			     (flag & SBI_PMU_STOP_FLAG_RESET)))
 			return ret;
 
 		if (cidx > (CSR_INSTRET - CSR_CYCLE) && flag & SBI_PMU_STOP_FLAG_RESET) {
